Title: Worktree GC belongs to lair only — roar/molt killed live session worktrees
Created: 2026-07-14
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-07-14T21:40Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context
On 2026-07-13 the worktree GC (`scripts/worktree-gc.sh`) removed two
worktrees that were live sessions' base cwds:

- 09:15Z — a roar wrap-up removed `explore-session` while four sessions
  were active in it.
- 19:35Z — a roar in `t-gaze-fork-liveness` removed
  `explore-ticket-pipeline` while one session was mid-flight and two more
  had started in it two minutes earlier.

The GC's staleness test (upstream `[gone]` + clean tree) reads "branch
merged" as "worktree unused". A session-base worktree whose checked-out
branch was pushed and merged (PRs #554/#555 merged from
`worktree-explore-ticket-pipeline`; deleteBranchOnMerge pruned the remote)
becomes GC-bait while sessions still sit in it. Removal strands them in
unregistered husk dirs where git resolves to the primary checkout —
roughly eight sessions ran unisolated in the `explore-ticket-pipeline`
husk over the following two days.

Author ruling: roar and molt do nothing outside their isolated
environment, and never worktree GC. That cleanup belongs to /lair, the
end-of-day wrap-up.

## Relevant files
- `skills/roar/SKILL.md` — step 10 (GC) removed; steps renumbered
- `skills/molt/SKILL.md` — step 1.5 (GC) replaced by a no-GC note
- `skills/lair/SKILL.md` — step 8 hygiene sweep now owns the GC, with a
  no-other-live-session precondition
- `scripts/worktree-gc.sh` — unchanged here; residual hardening below

## Exit criteria
- [ ] roar SKILL.md no longer invokes `worktree-gc.sh`
- [ ] molt SKILL.md no longer invokes `worktree-gc.sh`
- [ ] lair SKILL.md invokes it with the live-session precondition
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Residual (not this ticket)
The script itself still lacks a liveness rail and unlocks locked
worktrees before removing them (`worktree-gc.sh:49-57`), so even a
lair-time GC can hit a live overnight background session. The
`explore-ticket-pipeline` husk also still hosts live sessions and needs
repair. Follow-up if judged worthwhile; see tickets 0325/0338 for husks.
